    "status": "SUCCESS",
    "response": "In short: **no, you should not read into the fact that the panel was made up entirely of women, nor is it likely a sign of unfair bias against you.** \n\nHere is an analysis of why, along with what is more likely driving the outcome and how to proceed:\n\n---\n\n### 1. Why the Panel's Gender Composition Isn't a Red Flag\n* **Both you and the successful candidate are women:** Because the role was awarded to another woman from the shortlist, an allegation or suspicion of sex discrimination would be virtually impossible to substantiate.\n* **Reflective of internal stakeholders:** Senior selection panels at banks typically consist of key cross-functional partners—such as the Chief Risk Officer, Head of Compliance, Head of Model Risk Governance, and an HR Business Partner. In modern risk and compliance divisions, having multiple women in these senior roles is increasingly common. It is far more likely a reflection of who holds those leadership seats than an intentional or coordinated posture.\n\n---\n\n### 2. The Likely Decision Driver: Technical Depth vs. Regulatory Strategy\nFor a **Head of Risk Modelling** role at a bank, hiring priorities often diverge sharply from individual contributor or lead quantitative roles:\n\n* **Your profile:** A PhD in statistics with 9 years of risk modelling represents exceptional technical and quantitative strength.\n* **The selected profile:** A master's in finance with \"broad experience of regulatory affairs\" suggests a background centered on navigating regulators, compliance, and governance.\n\nIn banking, model risk functions operate under intense regulatory oversight (e.g., Fed/OCC SR 11-7, ECB TRIM, PRA standards). Boards and executive panels frequently prioritize:\n* Defending models to external regulators, internal audit, and the board.\n* Navigating policy, governance frameworks, and regulatory inspections.\n* Executive stakeholder management and translation of complex models into business decisions.\n\nThe panel likely favored someone with proven experience managing regulatory interfaces over greater mathematical depth, assuming the team's underlying quants already provide the statistical firepower.\n\n---\n\n### 3. The Lack of Written Feedback After Two Weeks\n* **Legal caution:** Most corporate and banking HR departments explicitly advise interviewers **against** providing written explanations, as written feedback can be scrutinized for legal liability.\n* **Internal delays:** Two weeks is standard corporate latency for post-interview debriefs, especially when offer negotiations or contract finalizations with the primary candidate are ongoing.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Recommended Next Steps\n\n1. **Shift from written feedback to a verbal debrief:**\n   Written requests often trigger HR stonewalling. Follow up with the recruiter or hiring manager proposing a brief 10–15 minute phone call instead:\n   > *\"I understand you may not be able to provide a formal written debrief, but I would greatly value a brief 10-minute call to hear any guidance on how I can position myself for future executive/head-level opportunities at the bank.\"*\n2. **Focus questions on leadership and governance:**\n   If you secure the call, ask:\n   * *“Were there specific governance or regulatory competencies you felt the successful candidate brought that I should look to develop?”*\n   * *“How did the panel view the balance between technical modelling depth versus regulatory/stakeholder management for this role?”*\n3. **Frame your narrative for future 'Head of' roles:**\n   For future applications at this level, ensure your CV and interview examples emphasize your experience with model validation standards, regulatory audits, and cross-functional leadership alongside your statistical expertise.\n",
